# RME clinical workflow integrity (rule 223)

**Status:** GO scope of `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-5` (2026-11-06). Sprint record:
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-5.md`. Owner approval: the mission brief of 2026-11-06 authorised an audit of
the clinical workflow CW-01..CW-11 and the correction of VERIFIED defects only, recorded in the sprint doc (CG-22).
Category under the Foundation-First Sprint Lock: clinical correctness / security fix; the lock is unchanged.

This rule EXTENDS, and supersedes nothing in, rules 109, 134, 135 and 199. Where it narrows a statement in one of
them, the narrowing is stated below and the older rule carries a pointer here.

## 1. A transition is decided on the locked row (CW5-01)

1. `ClinicVisitService::transitionStatus()` takes `ClinicVisitRepositoryInterface::lockForStatusDecision()` (FOR NO
   KEY UPDATE) on the visit inside its transaction and re-evaluates `ClinicVisit::VALID_TRANSITIONS` (and, for
   `cashier_pending`, the signed consent) on THAT row before writing; the write goes to the locked row and the
   caller's instance is refreshed.
1a. Lock strength is FOR NO KEY UPDATE, never FOR UPDATE: it conflicts with itself and with FOR UPDATE (so it
   serialises against another transition, `void()` and `RmePerformedTreatmentService`), but not with the FOR KEY
   SHARE every foreign-key INSERT takes on the visit — diagnoses, payments, consents and odontograms written against
   the visit are never blocked and cannot deadlock with it (the 40P01 `RmeInvoiceService::create()` records).
2. The early checks on the caller's copy stay (they give the fast, familiar refusal); they are never the authority.
3. Why: two requests overlapping by milliseconds both read `in_progress` and both passed the map; the last commit
   won. A cancelled visit came back `cashier_pending` (billable, with `cancelled_at` still set), or a visit already
   at the cashier became `cancelled` — an edge the map does not have. Reproduced deterministically by acting on a
   stale instance after another write committed.
4. `assignRoom()` re-checks `isTerminal()` on the locked row: a cancel that committed in the meantime never
   receives a room, a doctor and a patient–doctor assignment.
5. `RmeVisitConsentService::sign()` takes the same visit lock first and re-asserts `assertSignable()` on the locked
   row BEFORE any signature file is written, so a cancel or finish committed in the meantime leaves neither a consent
   nor an orphaned image.
6. Lock order is one row, the visit, first — the row `void()` and `RmePerformedTreatmentService` lock. Never lock
   another clinical row before the visit inside these paths.
6a. Known residual (not changed): the caller's abilities (`transition`, `completeExamination`) are asked on the
   pre-lock copy; only status and consent are re-decided under the lock. A concurrent double submit now gets a
   visible "transition not allowed" refusal instead of silently re-writing `started_at`.
7. This closes rule 199 §27b (`transitionStatus(completed)` without a row lock).

## 2. Every RME authoring control mirrors the capability (CW5-02..04)

Extends rule 135 and AIR-RMC-25 to the surfaces the 2026-09 fix did not reach:

8. Detail Kunjungan "Buat Rekam Medis": the controller passes `canCreateRecordHere` — true only when
   `RmeVisitConsentService::resolveAuthorizedEncounter()` returns THIS visit. Otherwise the button renders disabled
   with the reason and there is no `<form>`.
9. Rekam Medis page "Selesai Pemeriksaan": offered only with the `transition` ability (doctor room guard),
   `completeExamination` and a signed consent — the same three facts Detail Kunjungan asks.
10. Rekam Medis page handwriting editor, "+ Tambah Halaman RM" and the structured-diagnosis forms follow
    `canAuthorRme`, never `can('update', $medicalRecord)` alone.
11. When the encounter IS consented but this account may not author (§3), the page says so ("Hanya dapat dibaca");
    it never tells the user to fetch a consent that already exists.

## 3. The front office never authors the clinical record (CW5-05)

12. `FrontOfficeRole::isVisitFrontDeskOnly()` is the single definition of the tier (`Admin Klinik`, `Front Office`
    without a clinical role). `ClinicVisitPolicy` and `RmeVisitConsentService::actorMayWorkOnEncounter()` both read it
    (and, since rule 226, `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority`).
13. A front-desk-only account is refused the active RME and the active odontogram at the canonical authority, which
    every authoring assertion and every capability predicate reads (RME, odontogram, performed treatments). The
    tier keeps everything it does: registration, queue, room placement, consent signing, cashier work, reading.
14. Holding a clinical role (`Doctor`, `Perawat`, `Supervisor RME`, `Super Admin`) as well keeps full authority.
15. Why: `manage_clinic_visits` is the registration permission the front office needs, and holding it was enough to
    chart the odontogram and write the RME on a consented encounter by direct request — against rule 134 §1 ("Doctor
    authorization"), §7 ("non-Doctor still refused"), rule 108 §12 and DPT-064.
16. NOT decided here (recorded as owner decisions in the sprint doc): whether `Perawat` / `Supervisor RME` may author,
    and whether the front office may start or cancel an examination. Do not widen §13 to "non-Doctor" without one.
16a. EXTENDED by rule 226 (DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8A, P8-N1): the prescription, its R/ canvas and its doctor-signature
    canvas are under §13 as well — the front-desk-only tier never writes them, and writes are scoped to the working
    branch. Single definition: `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority`. §16 is unchanged.

## 4. A new sheet's source visit is validated (CW5-06)

17. `MedicalRecordController::store()` resolves `source_visit_id` through
    `PatientRmWorkspaceResolver::resolveSourceVisit()` (same patient, RME branch) before storing it or echoing it into
    the redirect; anything else falls back to the visit in the URL. Never store a request-supplied visit id raw: a
    foreign patient's visit was recorded as a sheet's source, and a nonexistent id was an FK violation (HTTP 500).

## 5. Messages state the real rule (CW5-08)

18. The consent create refusal states the signing window (only while the examination runs); the success message
    says the RECORD may now be written, never that payment may proceed (AIR-RMC-11).

## 6. Testing

19. `tests/Feature/RME/RmeClinicalWorkflowIntegrityTest.php` pins all of the above, is selected by the
    `RmeClinicalWorkflowIntegrity` token in BOTH critical-gate variants and is declared in `config/ci_runner.php`
    `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`. Race tests act on a stale instance after another write committed (the exact
    interleaving of two requests); the lock itself is asserted on PostgreSQL (`FOR NO KEY UPDATE` on `trx_clinic_visits`, CW5-01f; this line said
    `FOR UPDATE` until DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-7 corrected the drift — §1a is the authority).
